g i  couldn't keep going was like the endless  amounts of energy that she had  she was always like performing so  you know energetically and at a 10 or  beyond a 10 and by the time i'd get home  you know i would do everything i can to  be present for you know with my family  but then i just  i was the second i could get i would  just crash and go to sleep because it  was really it was like pulling from a  well that of energy that i didn't think  was humanly possible  uh i'm always curious how actors get  ready for a big emotional scene or a  very dramatic scene where you know it's  really going to take it out of you and  you have to be present in the moment so  hypothetically speaking if you were  filming something like that on a monday  how can you take me through your process  and like are you thinking about it days  in advance is it the day before that  you're breaking down what you're going  to do like how do you get ready for  something like that  well that's  i was kind of like in that the whole  time because  you know what made her  a  joke and this is what a lot of the  comedy sketch you know tv shows made fun  of her for is that she cried a lot you  know she was very open and um very  vulnerable and she didn't really have a  barrier between her and others so if  someone said something that moved her  she was there with them and she was  emotional and so i kind of had to do  that to play her like just everything i  could to just  be as open as possible um so i wasn't  thinking in terms of like okay i have to  cry on this day i was more of like  um thinking in terms of just constantly  staying as open as i could i mean i did  more for me was the singing the crying i  don't you know  i feel like i if i'm in my head with the  character's journey i don't really need  to put substitutions in i can be in the  head of the character and then just go  where i need to go  but the singing was very stressful for  me and especially the last scene of the  movie because  t